@* FR-117's two states, named once.

   A shift is running or it is finished, and both screens say so - two copies of one vocabulary
   otherwise, and the kind of duplication where the second copy is the one that goes stale.

   StatusLabel's other face. The shape is the same shape a delivery status wears, deliberately: a
   reader learns one vocabulary rather than two. What must not converge is the words. The two driver
   pickers say "на зміні" and "не на зміні", which are claims about where somebody is now; this
   labels a row in a history, where last Tuesday's shift is finished and says nothing about whether
   its driver is working today. Sharing one pair of keys would have put a present-tense claim about
   a person in a column headed "стан зміни", and it would have been false for every row but the open
   one - which is why a driver's duty is a plain Badge and never this.

   Independent @@if blocks rather than a ternary or an if/else chain: an `else` at markup level reads
   to NFR-14's source scan as a rendered English word, and a key composed at run time would be a key
   the closed-catalogue test cannot see. The two literal localizer lookups below are what make those
   two keys reachable. *@
@inject IStringLocalizer<UiText> Localizer

@if (IsOpen)
{
    <DtStatusLabel Face="DtStatusFace.Running">@Localizer["ShiftRunning"]</DtStatusLabel>
}
@if (!IsOpen)
{
    <DtStatusLabel Face="DtStatusFace.Finished">@Localizer["ShiftFinished"]</DtStatusLabel>
}

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// Whether the shift is still running. A bool rather than a status enum, because there is no
    /// status column to mirror: open is <c>EndedAt IS NULL</c>, which is also the filter of the
    /// index enforcing one open shift per driver (FR-110, FR-117).
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    [EditorRequired]
    public bool IsOpen { get; set; }
}
